            <ArticleTitle>Q&amp;A: The brain under a mesoscope: the forest and the trees.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>82</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1186/s12915-017-0426-y</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Neurons relevant to a particular behavior are often widely dispersed across the brain. To record activity in groups of individual neurons that might be distributed across large distances, neuroscientists and optical engineers have been developing a new type of microscope called a mesoscope. Mesoscopes have high spatial resolution and a large field of view. This Q&amp;A will discuss this exciting new technology, highlighting a particular instrument, the two-photon random access mesoscope (2pRAM).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
